questions.stem:
A class stores a private final java.util.List<String> and offers this constructor:
Bag(java.util.List<String> src) { items = src; }
After it runs, the caller keeps src and calls src.add("late"). What happens to the bag?

A class stores a private final java.util.List<String> and builds it as:
items = java.util.Collections.unmodifiableList(src);
where src is the caller's list. The class promises the caller can never change items. Which claim is true?

A running log holds private final StringBuilder text and exposes:
public StringBuilder text() { return text; }
A caller runs log.text().append("hello"). What is true afterwards?

A class declares Value(String label) and Value(Object payload). A client writes:
Value v = new Value(null);
What does the compiler do?

A class declares Value(String label) and Value(Integer size). A client writes:
Value v = new Value(null);
The compiler reports an ambiguous reference. What is the correct account?

A class declares, in this order:
static int trace = 0;
static final Door DEFAULT = make(1);
static { trace = trace + 10; }
where make adds 1 to trace and returns a Door. What is trace once class setup finishes?

An outer class declares int v = 5. Its inner class declares its own int v = 7 and a method:
int both() { return v + Outer.this.v; }
What does both() return?

class Trace {
  static StringBuilder log = new StringBuilder();
  static int s1 = mark("s1");
  static { mark("sb"); }
  int i1 = mark("i1");
  { mark("ib"); }
  Trace() { mark("ctor"); }
  static int mark(String t) { log.append(t).append(" "); return 0; }
}
new Trace();
new Trace();
System.out.print(log.toString().trim());
What is printed?

class Watcher { static void note(Row r) { System.out.print(r.id + "-" + r.tag); } }
class Row {
  int id; String tag;
  Row(int id, String tag) { Watcher.note(this); this.id = id; this.tag = tag; }
}
new Row(4, "aisle");
What is printed?

class P {
  int x, y;
  P(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }
  public boolean equals(Object o) { return (o instanceof P p) && p.x == x && p.y == y; }
}
P a = new P(1, 2);
P b = new P(1, 2);
java.util.HashSet<P> s = new java.util.HashSet<>();
s.add(a);
System.out.print(a.equals(b) + " " + s.contains(b));
What is printed?

class Money {
  int cents;
  Money(int c) { cents = c; }
  public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (o == null || getClass() != o.getClass()) { return false; }
    return cents == ((Money) o).cents;
  }
}
class Cash extends Money {
  String note;
  Cash(int c, String n) { super(c); note = n; }
  public boolean equals(Object o) { return (o instanceof Money m) && m.cents == cents; }
}
System.out.print(new Money(100).equals(new Cash(100, "n")) + " "
              + new Cash(100, "n").equals(new Money(100)));
What is printed?

class Mix {
  int id, slot;
  Mix(int id, int slot) { this.id = id; this.slot = slot; }
  public boolean equals(Object o) { return (o instanceof Mix m) && m.id == id; }
  public int hashCode() { return slot; }
}
java.util.HashMap<Mix, String> m = new java.util.HashMap<>();
m.put(new Mix(1, 10), "slot-a");
System.out.print(m.get(new Mix(1, 99)));
What is printed?

class Shelf {
  private final java.util.List<String> items = new java.util.ArrayList<>();
  void add(String s) { items.add(s); }
  java.util.List<String> view() { return java.util.Collections.unmodifiableList(items); }
}
Shelf shelf = new Shelf();
shelf.add("a");
java.util.List<String> kept = shelf.view();
shelf.add("b");
System.out.print(kept.size() + " " + kept.get(1));
What is printed?

class Note {
  private final StringBuilder text = new StringBuilder();
  public StringBuilder text() { return text; }
  int length() { return text.length(); }
}
Note n = new Note();
n.text().append("hello");
System.out.print(n.length());
What is printed?

class V {
  V(int n) { System.out.print("int "); }
  V(Integer n) { System.out.print("Integer "); }
  V(Object o) { System.out.print("Object "); }
}
byte b = 1;
new V(b);
new V(Integer.valueOf(3));
new V(5L);
What is printed?

class Tag {
  Tag(int id) { System.out.print("int "); }
  Tag(Integer id) { System.out.print("Integer "); }
  Tag(Object id) { System.out.print("Object "); }
}
new Tag(null);
What is printed?

class Ticket {
  static int last = 0;
  final int number;
  Ticket(int cap) {
    last = last + 1;
    if (cap <= 0) { throw new IllegalArgumentException("cap"); }
    number = last;
  }
}
new Ticket(5);
try { new Ticket(0); } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) { }
System.out.print(new Ticket(7).number);
What is printed?

class Chip {
  final int pins; final String label;
  Chip(int pins) { this(pins, "none"); }
  Chip(int pins, String label) { this.pins = pins; this.label = label; }
  Chip(Chip o) { this(o.pins); }
  String show() { return pins + "/" + label; }
}
Chip original = new Chip(8, "led");
System.out.print(new Chip(original).show());
What is printed?

class Crate {
  final java.util.List<String> tags;
  Crate(java.util.List<String> t) { tags = new java.util.ArrayList<String>(t); }
  Crate(Crate o) { tags = o.tags; }
}
Crate a = new Crate(java.util.Arrays.asList("t"));
Crate b = new Crate(a);
b.tags.add("extra");
System.out.print(a.tags.size() + " " + b.tags.size());
What is printed?

class Pnt {
  final int x, y;
  private Pnt(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }
  static class B {
    int x, y;
    B x(int v) { x = v; return this; }
    B y(int v) { y = v; return this; }
    Pnt build() { return new Pnt(x, y); }
  }
}
Pnt p = new Pnt.B().x(1).y(2).build();
Pnt q = new Pnt.B().x(1).y(2).build();
System.out.print((p == q) + " " + (p.x * 10 + p.y) + (q.x * 10 + q.y));
What is printed?

class Outer {
  int v = 5;
  class Inner {
    int v = 7;
    int both() { return v + Outer.this.v; }
    int mine() { return this.v; }
  }
}
Outer o = new Outer();
Outer.Inner i = o.new Inner();
System.out.print(i.both() + " " + i.mine());
What is printed?

A class declares Value(String label) and Value(Integer size), and a client writes:
Value v = new Value(null);
The compiler reports an ambiguous reference. What is wrong and what fixes it?

A class believes it has taken ownership of its data:
Bag(java.util.List<String> src) { items = java.util.Collections.unmodifiableList(src); }
Callers go on mutating their own list and the bag changes. What is wrong?

A money class is meant to compare by value but writes:
public boolean equals(Object o) { return this == o; }
public int hashCode() { return cents; }
Two separately built objects both holding 100 report unequal. Why?

A class compares only id and mixes a second field into its hash:
public boolean equals(Object o) { return (o instanceof Mix m) && m.id == id; }
public int hashCode() { return 31 * id + slot; }
A lookup with an otherwise equal instance keeps missing. Which change restores the contract?

A class is documented as immutable and declares private final java.util.List<String> tags, yet it exposes:
public java.util.List<String> tags() { return tags; }
Which claim exposes the contradiction?

public final class Bag {
  private final java.util.List<String> items;
  public Bag(java.util.List<String> src) { items = ____; }
}
Fill the blank so the bag keeps its own copy and later changes by the caller cannot reach it.

class Rack {
  private final java.util.List<String> items = new java.util.ArrayList<String>();
  void add(String s) { items.add(s); }
  public java.util.List<String> items() {
    return ____;
  }
}
Callers must see every later addition the rack makes, but must not be able to change the list.

class Money {
  final long cents;
  private Money(long c) { cents = c; }
  static class B {
    private long cents = -1;
    B cents(long c) { cents = c; return this; }
    Money build() {
      if (cents < 0) { throw new IllegalStateException("cents required"); }
      return ____;
    }
  }
}
Fill the blank so build returns the value object the settings describe.

class Money {
  final long cents;
  private Money(long c) { this.cents = c; }
  public static Money ofCents(long cents) {
    ____
    return new Money(cents);
  }
}
Fill the blank so ofCents rejects a negative amount instead of building one.

class Width { ____ int width; Width(int w) { width = w; } }
class Helper { static int twice(Width v) { return v.width * 2; } }
Helper sits in the same package, the goal is the narrowest visibility that still works, and no class may rewrite width. Fill the blank.

class Tag {
  private final String code;
  Tag(String code) { this.code = code; }
  public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (!(o instanceof Tag other)) { return false; }
    return ____;
  }
  public int hashCode() { return code.hashCode(); }
}
Fill the blank so two tags are equal when their codes match.

class Crate {
  final int size; final java.util.List<String> tags;
  Crate(int size, java.util.List<String> tags) { this.size = size; this.tags = tags; }
  Crate(Crate o) { size = o.size; tags = ____; }
}
Fill the blank so the copy does not share its list with the original.

class Tag {
  private final String code; private final int seq;
  Tag(String code, int seq) { this.code = code; this.seq = seq; }
  public boolean equals(Object o) { return (o instanceof Tag t) && t.code.equals(code); }
  public int hashCode() { return ____; }
}
Fill the blank so a lookup with an equal tag finds the stored one in a hash map.

A class fails to compile, and the compiler says constructor Point(int,int) is already defined in class Point for:
class Point { int x, y; Point(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; } Point(int y, int x) { this.x = x; this.y = y; } }
What is the real cause?

Two builders of a record type take unrelated types:
class Value { Value(String label) { } Value(Integer size) { } }
A client writes Value v = new Value(null); and the compiler reports reference to Value is ambiguous, noting that both constructors match. What is the fix?
